  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nBroad area of low pressure over the area this afternoon, with a warm \nfront splayed out across the CWA. Further west, surface cold front \nis nearing the MT/ND border. Stratus clouds north of the warm front \nare slowly eroding this afternoon, and will continue to do so over \nthe next couple hours. Already beginning to see a growing cumulus \nfield across the western Dakotas within the surface trough. Also \nfurther west across eastern MT is an approaching broken line of \nshowers/storms associated more in line with the forcing from the \nupper trough. Mesoscale discussion already posted for the western \nDakotas for severe storms potentially developing/moving into the \nregion over the next couple hours. CAMs continue to suggest a broken \nline of convection gradually forming and moving eastward into the \ncentral Dakotas. Areal coverage still appears greatest over ND, but \nensemble paintball output does show the southern portion of the line \nof convection into central/north central SD this evening. Big \nquestion is just how far south and east convection will survive as \nwe progress through the evening hours. It appears by the time we're \ndealing with convection in our western CWA, the main threats will be \nstrong wind gusts between 60 and 75 mph, although stronger cores \ncould contain hail from quarter to golf ball size. \n\nOn Monday, post-frontal air mass moves in, which will feature much \ndrier conditions with dewpoints dropping down into the 50s. Winds \nlook to be rather light as well as high pressure settles in. \nAlthough highs still look to be in the 80s, it will feel much more \ncomfortable with the lack of humidity. RH values do fall to between \n25 and 30 percent west of the Missouri, but with a lack of strong \nwinds, fire weather concerns shouldn't be surfacing too much.\n\nFocus will then shift to Tuesday as shortwave energy moves into the \nregion from the southwest and precipitation chances return. Low-\nlevel moisture will be on the increase once again with dewpoints \nshooting back into the 60s, especially over the southern/southeast \nCWA. Models continue to show precipitation over the region Tuesday \nafternoon and evening, and inherited PoPs are fairly high (50-70%). \nStill seeing a signal for heavy rainfall potential with NBM 90th \npercentile 24hr QPF between 1 to 2 inches. ML severe storm potential \noutput for Day 3 (Tuesday) shows low end (5-15%) probabilities \nacross portions of the CWA.",
